vo_avfoundation: stamp tvOS host frames 30% into a refresh

After a seek the video layer and the OSD layer can settle on different
refreshes for samples stamped late in a refresh interval, and stay a
refresh apart until the next pause or seek. A host stamp is mpv's target
time, so where it falls in the interval is set anew at every pause and
seek: after some seeks the subtitles ran a refresh late for the whole
stretch of playback, at any queue lead (plezy#3073).

The display link of patch 0054 now also gives the latest vsync, and a
host stamp moves to the nearest point 30% of the way through a refresh
interval, by at most half a refresh either way; on average a frame shows
on the refresh it showed on before. Android's ExoPlayer places frames the
same way (VideoFrameReleaseHelper: the nearest vsync, released 80% of a
refresh before it). iOS and macOS track no vsync and keep their stamps.

Apple TV 4K, Plezy's options, a clip whose frames and per-frame ASS
events carry the same code in both halves of the picture, captured over
HDMI. Patch 0054 alone, as published: in 9 runs at 23.976 Hz, a pause or
a seek every 3 s, 5 stretches after a seek had the subtitles a refresh
late throughout, with the same 154-158 ms of lead as the rest. Stamps
held at one place in the interval, 20 seeks a run and 2.5 s of playback
after each: at 23.976 Hz 18 of 38 stretches so at 75%, none of 27 at 30%;
at 50 Hz (25 fps) 6 of 20 at 75%, 3 of 20 at 90%, none of 20 at each of
10%, 30% and 50%.

With this patch, stamps where mpv's targets put them: every stamp of
steady playback sat at 30% (1st to 99th percentile) in every run, frames
went to the layers at least 3.6 refreshes ahead, and no capture of steady
playback mismatched. At 23.976 Hz 222 stretches, 136 of them after a seek
(20 seeks a run, a pause or a seek every 3 s, heavy subtitles, the switch
made before the first frame, an 11-minute session of pauses and seeks);
at 50 Hz 147 (25 and 23.976 fps); at 59.94 Hz 84 (29.97 and 23.976 fps).
In 15 minutes of uninterrupted playback every stamp came one refresh after
the last. Left: the frame a pause stops on, and the first five frames
after a seek, where the subtitles can trail the burst of frames a seek
starts with by up to two frames for about 170 ms.

